WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.264
So much of our communication is about
motivation, motivating others and

00:00:05.264 --> 00:00:07.440
motivating ourselves.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.571
I'm Matt Abrahams and

00:00:08.571 --> 00:00:12.800
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.100
Welcome to Think Fast ,Talk Smart,
The Podcast.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.873
I am really excited to
speak with Szu-chi Huang,

00:00:19.873 --> 00:00:23.590
an Associate Professor of
Marketing at Stanford GSB.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:28.600
Szu-chi's research focuses on motivation
science and shared goal pursuit.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:34.493
She studies things such as competition,
healthy eating, and dynamic feedback.

00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:36.736
[MUSIC]

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:38.980
Hi, Szu-chi, welcome to the podcast.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.480
I am super excited to have you here.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.060
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Matt.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.220
It's so good to be here.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.730
&gt;&gt; Excellent,
let's go ahead and get started.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.817
In preparing to speak with you,
I reviewed a lot of your work.

00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:53.709
And I'm struck by the variety of your
research, from advertising to robots and

00:00:53.709 --> 00:00:55.720
goals and competition.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.130
I'm curious,
what motivates the research you do?

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.912
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that is a great question to start
with because definitely I have pretty

00:01:02.912 --> 00:01:04.140
broad interests.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.789
I actually started getting intrigued
by the topic related to motivation

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:13.218
because I was working at an advertising
agency, JWT back in Taiwan.

00:01:13.218 --> 00:01:17.419
And at that time in advertising,
the biggest question is how do we motivate

00:01:17.419 --> 00:01:21.165
our customers to come back to
the store to purchase more products?

00:01:21.165 --> 00:01:24.358
And I realized we don't really
have a lot of answers about that.

00:01:24.358 --> 00:01:28.002
And that eventually motivated
me to join academia, and

00:01:28.002 --> 00:01:32.901
therefore, I do a lot of projects
related to motivation and goal pursuit.

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:36.981
Talking about how people's motivation
could change from one stage to another or

00:01:36.981 --> 00:01:39.023
how we could potentially collaborate or

00:01:39.023 --> 00:01:42.190
compete when we are working
on goals together.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.454
And then because of that,
through my students,

00:01:44.454 --> 00:01:47.339
I started branching out to look
at other types of motivation.

00:01:47.339 --> 00:01:51.871
Such as how do we motivate donors to
donate money or to volunteer, and

00:01:51.871 --> 00:01:56.092
also looking at how new technologies
shape our motivation, for

00:01:56.092 --> 00:01:59.397
instance, the use of FinTech,
service robots.

00:01:59.397 --> 00:02:03.303
All these new technologies can affect
our motivation in the sense that it can

00:02:03.303 --> 00:02:05.867
increase our motivation to
do the things we love or

00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:07.837
sometimes decrease our motivation.

00:02:07.837 --> 00:02:10.760
And that's why I started
looking into these other areas.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.624
&gt;&gt; It's so exciting the things you study.

00:02:12.624 --> 00:02:16.856
And I love that the story comes from your
own curiosity and need when you were

00:02:16.856 --> 00:02:21.239
doing advertising to see that we just
didn't know enough about motivation.

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:24.821
And we'll get to talking about
motivation soon, for sure.

00:02:24.821 --> 00:02:29.291
We did a series of podcast episodes
where we talked about communication, and

00:02:29.291 --> 00:02:33.574
decision making, and decision science
with some of our GSB colleagues.

00:02:33.574 --> 00:02:35.862
And you teach a class,
and I love the name.

00:02:35.862 --> 00:02:38.572
It's called The Brains and
Guts of Decision Making.

00:02:38.572 --> 00:02:42.091
Can you share one or two key ideas
that you cover in that class?

00:02:42.091 --> 00:02:45.488
&gt;&gt; Definitely, so The Brains and
Guts of Decision Making,

00:02:45.488 --> 00:02:47.555
I teach that with Jonathan Levav.

00:02:47.555 --> 00:02:49.965
So we are co-directors of the program.

00:02:49.965 --> 00:02:52.131
So he takes care of the brain part and

00:02:52.131 --> 00:02:56.044
I take care of the gut part because
I'm a motivation scientist.

00:02:56.044 --> 00:02:58.186
And the two main takeaways I have for

00:02:58.186 --> 00:03:02.790
students in that class is the first
thing is that it's all about needs.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.448
So in order to think about how
we shape people's behavior,

00:03:05.448 --> 00:03:08.380
it's about understanding
what needs they have.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.999
And oftentimes when we think about
a company or a product, we can easily zoom

00:03:12.999 --> 00:03:18.240
into one need we believe people have,
but it's actually way richer than that.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.964
So I give them an example of Nike.

00:03:19.964 --> 00:03:24.385
Nike sells shoes, and shoes satisfy
one need, physiological need.

00:03:24.385 --> 00:03:27.289
So we don't have to hurt
our foot when we walk.

00:03:27.289 --> 00:03:29.073
But of course, that's way more than that.

00:03:29.073 --> 00:03:32.538
Nike also sells self-esteem needs,
social needs so

00:03:32.538 --> 00:03:35.008
you can jog with others, share data.

00:03:35.008 --> 00:03:38.947
Self-actualization needs because
through jogging, you become fitter,

00:03:38.947 --> 00:03:40.369
closer to your ideal self.

00:03:40.369 --> 00:03:42.570
So the needs can be all kinds of needs.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.451
And it's about picking up the right
motivation to speak to your target

00:03:46.451 --> 00:03:47.540
audience.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.280
So that's the first takeaway usually
I hope our students will have.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.990
The second one is closely tied to
needs and motivation is emotionality.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.606
Because when our needs are satisfied,
we get happy, so

00:03:58.606 --> 00:04:01.010
there's a lot of positive emotion there.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.808
But when they're dissatisfied, that's
when we get really mad and grumpy, so

00:04:04.808 --> 00:04:07.140
we have a lot of negative emotion.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:12.590
Recent research showed that emotionality
is really key in all our decision making.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.478
Researchers were able to find that
emotionality behind the reviews actually

00:04:16.478 --> 00:04:20.030
account for way more variance
than the content of the reviews.

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:24.456
So they were able to actually link the
emotionality behind the reviews to predict

00:04:24.456 --> 00:04:27.775
the success of thousands of movies,
billions of books, and

00:04:27.775 --> 00:04:29.610
restaurant reservations.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.770
So it's not so much about what
the reviewers actually write about, but

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:36.440
the emotionality behind their reviews
that drives people's decision.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.510
And so motivation, emotionality, these
are the two things I want to highlight.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:44.846
&gt;&gt; Wow, well, when I look at my Nike shoes
sitting in the corner and the fact that I

00:04:44.846 --> 00:04:49.470
haven't run in them, it sometimes make me
feel bad, but I find that fascinating.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.280
So when we talk on this podcast a lot
about knowing your audience, and

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.067
we talk a lot about their needs.

00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:57.028
And many of us when we
start our communication,

00:04:57.028 --> 00:04:58.309
we start from the wrong place.

00:04:58.309 --> 00:05:01.971
We start from what do we want to say
rather than thinking about the needs.

00:05:01.971 --> 00:05:05.408
And what I like about what you've said
is you have to uncover all of the needs.

00:05:05.408 --> 00:05:08.889
It might not just be one thing,
and that's fascinating.

00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:12.857
And the other aspect you said about
the emotionality, so it's not even so

00:05:12.857 --> 00:05:16.889
much what you say, but it's the emotion
behind it that we're decoding and

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:18.757
reading and can be motivational.

00:05:18.757 --> 00:05:19.930
Really, really interesting.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:25.376
And I find that to be true for myself when
I read a review of a restaurant or a book.

00:05:25.376 --> 00:05:28.380
I am taking away,
how did that person feel about that?

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.040
&gt;&gt; Definitely, and it's really fundamental
because that's how human beings connect.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.560
And that's why we categorize
it as the gut reaction.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.820
That's a lot of times the key indicator of
our perception, attitude, and behavior.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:39.626
&gt;&gt; That's great,

00:05:39.626 --> 00:05:43.030
and Jonathan was on recently and
he shared fantastic insights, too.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.700
I can only imagine your students take
away amazing things from both of you.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:51.240
You also did research that looked at
what you call information avoidance.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.484
Can you share what your findings were and

00:05:53.484 --> 00:05:57.712
discuss how choosing to avoid some
information can affect our motivation?

00:05:57.712 --> 00:06:01.742
&gt;&gt; Definitely, this is actually one of
the most fascinating findings I have

00:06:01.742 --> 00:06:05.065
discovered, and
also one of the most personally relevant.

00:06:05.065 --> 00:06:08.805
So in the modern days, we know we
have all kinds of social information.

00:06:08.805 --> 00:06:12.599
I log into Facebook,
LinkedIn, Twitter, Instagram.

00:06:12.599 --> 00:06:15.960
I get to know what all my friends and
colleague are doing.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.443
And so it's really an era where we have
way more social information than we need.

00:06:20.443 --> 00:06:22.418
From the motivations perspective,

00:06:22.418 --> 00:06:25.730
what that means is we get all
kinds of social benchmarks.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:30.512
We know how much progress other people are
making on goals very similar to our own,

00:06:30.512 --> 00:06:34.270
could be health goals, family,
time goals, or career goals.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:39.778
And what we found in this research is that
we don't always want that information.

00:06:39.778 --> 00:06:42.889
So specifically, when we
are starting to work on a new goal,

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:44.298
we love that information.

00:06:44.298 --> 00:06:47.648
Because we want to learn from
what other people were doing and

00:06:47.648 --> 00:06:49.665
we want to learn from their success.

00:06:49.665 --> 00:06:51.916
Also as we get close to
actually reaching our goals,

00:06:51.916 --> 00:06:53.972
we are usually feeling
great about ourselves.

00:06:53.972 --> 00:06:56.839
So at that moment,
we don't mind having those informations.

00:06:56.839 --> 00:07:00.676
Because it can help us feel like we
are doing great and we are winning and

00:07:00.676 --> 00:07:01.725
that feels great.

00:07:01.725 --> 00:07:06.159
But what really is critical is halfway
through our goal pursuit process.

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:09.800
That is the moment where we
kind of we lose curiosity.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.760
We don't really feel we need to
learn more about the goal anymore.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:15.807
We are not fully confident that
we can achieve our goal yet, so

00:07:15.807 --> 00:07:17.770
we are not in the late stage.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.770
So that halfway point is when
we have a lot of insecurity.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.739
We are losing a little bit
motivation there, and that moment,

00:07:23.739 --> 00:07:26.270
we don't want to know how
well other people are doing.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.465
So that is when social information
becomes really threatening, and

00:07:29.465 --> 00:07:31.770
that is when we want to avoid it.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:35.574
But what we found is that if you give
people a choice to actually avoid

00:07:35.574 --> 00:07:38.313
information, they can
mute the notification,

00:07:38.313 --> 00:07:41.960
move their eyes away from
the source of information.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.110
What happens is they now don't have the
benchmark that actually motivates them.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:50.290
So information avoidance can actually
lead to decreased motivation.

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:54.140
And so after I found that, I used that
piece of research to remind myself.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:58.583
So whenever I work on my goals, and I feel
really stressed out, and I feel that other

00:07:58.583 --> 00:08:02.910
people are doing great better than me,
I want to avoid the information.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.628
Those are the moment I realized,
actually, I shouldn't be afraid.

00:08:05.628 --> 00:08:09.392
I should actually see those
information because that will give

00:08:09.392 --> 00:08:12.811
the benchmark I need to motivate
myself to move forward.

00:08:12.811 --> 00:08:17.343
So that is kind of the key learning
I personally have from that research.

00:08:17.343 --> 00:08:19.003
&gt;&gt; I so relate to that.

00:08:19.003 --> 00:08:21.188
I'm by nature a competitive person.

00:08:21.188 --> 00:08:25.066
And when I get really excited
with friends and others and we're

00:08:25.066 --> 00:08:29.840
going to go embark on this if it's doing
some kind of exercise or losing weight.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:33.252
And after I'd been doing it a little
while, I do get that insecurity.

00:08:33.252 --> 00:08:35.010
Can I keep this up as I kind of go?

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:38.673
And then when I see my friends making more
progress, and, of course, they're very

00:08:38.673 --> 00:08:42.044
proud to share that progress, it makes
me feel really bad and I do tune it out.

00:08:42.044 --> 00:08:45.599
But what I'm hearing you say is that
I should go against that desire to

00:08:45.599 --> 00:08:48.802
tune it out and actually use it
to keep that motivation going.

00:08:48.802 --> 00:08:53.184
&gt;&gt; What we found is that the moment that
we feel the most insecure about having

00:08:53.184 --> 00:08:57.778
social information is the moment where
those information really is valuable

00:08:57.778 --> 00:08:58.556
to have.

00:08:58.556 --> 00:09:00.005
&gt;&gt; I love counterintuitive things.

00:09:00.005 --> 00:09:02.393
And I'm going to try that
because I got some friends and

00:09:02.393 --> 00:09:05.092
we've got this little thing
around health going on.

00:09:05.092 --> 00:09:08.992
And I'm going to dive back in because
I've muted some of that information.

00:09:08.992 --> 00:09:10.098
So thank you for that.

00:09:10.098 --> 00:09:12.138
We'll see,
I'll check in with you in a little bit.

00:09:12.138 --> 00:09:13.415
We'll see how much it helped.

00:09:13.415 --> 00:09:16.087
&gt;&gt; I look forward to hearing
your progress on that, Matt.

00:09:16.087 --> 00:09:17.936
&gt;&gt; Okay, now I'm even more pressured.

00:09:17.936 --> 00:09:19.450
It's all good, it's all good.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:24.293
Can you share what your research suggests
are some concerns and best practices for

00:09:24.293 --> 00:09:26.770
designing motivating competitions?

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:31.206
&gt;&gt; So I have work that more directly look
at actual competition as zero-sum games,

00:09:31.206 --> 00:09:35.900
where there's an incentive or reward and
you have to beat others to get it.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:37.250
And based on the research,

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.580
we do know that competition
definitely increases motivation.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.250
It make attaining the goal more valuable.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:47.102
But what prior research hasn't looked
at is, is it better to be ahead or

00:09:47.102 --> 00:09:49.890
behind when you are in a competition?

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:54.124
So we actually look at all kinds of
data sources from working out to class

00:09:54.124 --> 00:09:55.370
performance.

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:58.948
And what we found is that both winning or
losing can motivate you,

00:09:58.948 --> 00:10:03.210
but they motivate you at different
phases of the competition.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:06.871
So in the beginning of the competition,
usually at that moment, we are looking for

00:10:06.871 --> 00:10:08.590
signal that we can actually win.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:11.180
So we are doing some kind
of probability assessment.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.723
If there's no chance of winning,

00:10:12.723 --> 00:10:16.360
we will just disengage instead of
investing more resources into it.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.511
So these kind of a winning signal or
feeling that you are ahead is so

00:10:19.511 --> 00:10:20.840
helpful in the beginning.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.379
If you are already losing in
the beginning, you will just give up.

00:10:25.379 --> 00:10:28.277
But winning signal doesn't always work.

00:10:28.277 --> 00:10:32.177
As you get closer to the late phase of
competition, if you are still winning,

00:10:32.177 --> 00:10:34.480
then the natural tendency is you relax.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.830
You feel like you don't
need to work hard anymore.

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:39.800
And we forget about the fact that
this is not an individual goal,

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:41.320
it's a competitive goal.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:44.787
And you have no idea what your
opponent will do in that last stage or

00:10:44.787 --> 00:10:46.770
last round of the game.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:51.030
So usually in the later phase,
being behind slightly is actually way more

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:55.870
motivating because it gives you
a very clear benchmark to achieve.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:59.120
So what do you do if you're actually
winning the whole way through?

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:04.130
What we did in one of the experiment is we
actually ran a used book donation drive.

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:08.150
It's a donation competition
across two campuses in China.

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:12.299
So you naturally have a winning campus
that is doing well and a losing campus.

00:11:12.299 --> 00:11:16.080
A few weeks in, they were
collecting books, but not so great.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:19.924
So what we did is, of course, we told
the losing campus that they are losing and

00:11:19.924 --> 00:11:21.390
they increase their effort.

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:26.410
But we also want to motivate the winning
campus to continue donating used books.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:29.579
So what we did is instead of
telling them they are winning,

00:11:29.579 --> 00:11:33.580
we compare them to their campus's
past performance last year.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:37.297
So yes, you are winning, but
comparing to how many books you were able

00:11:37.297 --> 00:11:41.120
to raise last year,
you are slightly behind of that benchmark.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.583
It is a self-comparison benchmark, but
turns out even that comparison can be

00:11:45.583 --> 00:11:49.718
meaningful to motivate the winning
campus to continue donating books.

00:11:49.718 --> 00:11:51.610
So that could be an important takeaway for

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:54.698
all of us who are listening to
the podcast who are winning right now.

00:11:54.698 --> 00:11:59.928
It's great to win and it sends a great
signal to help you stay motivated.

00:11:59.928 --> 00:12:04.167
But if you feel like you're actually
demotivated by that signal because it

00:12:04.167 --> 00:12:06.992
doesn't seem that you need
to put in more effort,

00:12:06.992 --> 00:12:11.045
maybe comparing to your past self
could be another way to motivate you.

00:12:11.045 --> 00:12:15.101
&gt;&gt; Wow, I really liked that there
are different points of comparison and

00:12:15.101 --> 00:12:17.727
different types of comparison to motivate.

00:12:17.727 --> 00:12:21.024
And as people who design competitions,
if you will,

00:12:21.024 --> 00:12:23.963
we can actually build in
some of these metrics.

00:12:23.963 --> 00:12:27.211
The information that we give to
people can be really interesting.

00:12:27.211 --> 00:12:31.473
I think of business leaders who might
be trying to motivate internal teams or

00:12:31.473 --> 00:12:33.214
across different companies.

00:12:33.214 --> 00:12:36.906
How we might give them feedback in
a way that keeps everybody motivated,

00:12:36.906 --> 00:12:39.282
regardless of if you're ahead or behind,
&gt;&gt; And

00:12:39.282 --> 00:12:42.190
we actually talk to companies a lot,
and also schools.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:46.530
You have students who are performing well
or sales teams who are performing well and

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:48.600
you really don't want them to relax.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:52.178
They should try to achieve more, but
you also can take away in the message that

00:12:52.178 --> 00:12:54.710
they are indeed winning
compared to their peers.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:58.119
So it is important to actually change
the benchmark for those people so

00:12:58.119 --> 00:13:00.500
they have something to strive for.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:04.309
&gt;&gt; Right, and one other point you made
that really stuck with me is if you feel

00:13:04.309 --> 00:13:06.910
like you're losing early on,
you just give up.

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:10.265
So that's an important time period to
give some motivational feedback so

00:13:10.265 --> 00:13:13.040
that you can keep people going,
because I've seen that happen.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:16.678
I've been an assistant coach
on some of my kids' teams.

00:13:16.678 --> 00:13:19.773
Or I've seen that in companies where I've
worked, where if people don't feel like

00:13:19.773 --> 00:13:22.009
they have the skills right off the bat,
they just check out.

00:13:22.009 --> 00:13:24.282
&gt;&gt; That's why we always say
that in the early stage,

00:13:24.282 --> 00:13:26.990
the most important signal
is attainability signal.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:29.444
People need to believe that
the goal is attainable or

00:13:29.444 --> 00:13:32.890
the competition is winnable so
they will continue working hard.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:36.920
And so you say, we can't just think about
giving the same feedback to everybody.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:40.224
For the people who really need that
attainability signal, we need to kind

00:13:40.224 --> 00:13:44.060
of give them different feedback so that we
encourage them from the perspective that,

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:47.110
yes, what you have can actually
help you achieve the goal.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:48.188
Stop comparing to other people.

00:13:48.188 --> 00:13:52.062
Maybe think about the ability you
already have learned or skills you have.

00:13:52.062 --> 00:13:55.402
So highlighting attainability is
going to be critical in the beginning.

00:13:55.402 --> 00:13:59.538
And somehow creating that benchmark,
that additional goal to strive for,

00:13:59.538 --> 00:14:04.180
additional comparison to strive for,
that'll be important at the end.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:08.407
&gt;&gt; It's striking me in several things
that you've shared with us that there

00:14:08.407 --> 00:14:12.292
are general principles, but
it comes down to really tailoring your

00:14:12.292 --> 00:14:17.287
motivational approach based on different
people's needs and their individuality.

00:14:17.287 --> 00:14:20.716
And I've heard you say that several times
and I think that's a really important

00:14:20.716 --> 00:14:23.698
takeaway for all of us is that
there's general guiding principles.

00:14:23.698 --> 00:14:25.807
But what you know about the individual or

00:14:25.807 --> 00:14:29.830
the team really drives the tailored
communication that you have to them.

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:33.567
&gt;&gt; The biggest part of my research finding
is on the temporal dynamics of goal

00:14:33.567 --> 00:14:34.350
pursuit.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.831
And the important aspect
of this is in the past,

00:14:36.831 --> 00:14:40.170
we tend to think about what
motivates people as a static thing.

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:44.670
So if I figure out one variable,
one factor, I just give that to everybody.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.580
But the temporal dynamics model
said that it should be dynamic.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:51.268
The feedback we give people should be
dynamic depending on who they are,

00:14:51.268 --> 00:14:54.361
what skills they think they have,
and where they come from.

00:14:54.361 --> 00:14:57.506
And also as they make more
progress during a competition or

00:14:57.506 --> 00:15:01.430
pursuing their personal goal,
the feedback should be dynamic as well.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.908
We can't keep on giving them
the same kind of signal.

00:15:03.908 --> 00:15:05.717
So changing that and tailor that for

00:15:05.717 --> 00:15:09.690
everybody's needs is going to be
critical in sustaining motivation.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.939
&gt;&gt; So this is a beautiful transition
to another question I have for you,

00:15:12.939 --> 00:15:16.523
which is this notion of what I hear you
say is your feedback has to be different

00:15:16.523 --> 00:15:19.010
based on where people
are in their journey.

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:23.170
And I know you along with our colleague
Jennifer Aaker have done some research

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:24.642
into how having a mindset,

00:15:24.642 --> 00:15:28.550
a journey mindset can actually really
influence how you achieve goals.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:29.743
Would you share more about that study?

00:15:29.743 --> 00:15:34.280
I found this really fundamentally
changing for me personally.

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:35.840
&gt;&gt; It was personal for me, too.

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:40.255
That research finding has a lot of
relevance for how I think about success.

00:15:40.255 --> 00:15:42.482
And definitely,
the motivation for that work,

00:15:42.482 --> 00:15:46.465
it came from the fact that we tend to
think about goal success as a destination.

00:15:46.465 --> 00:15:48.995
We reach the destination and
we feel great.

00:15:48.995 --> 00:15:52.368
But what happen when you reach the
destination is that there's nothing else

00:15:52.368 --> 00:15:53.510
left to be done.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:57.644
So a lot of times people graduate and they
stop learning, or they lose weight, and so

00:15:57.644 --> 00:15:59.430
they stop working out.

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:02.812
And fundamentally,
we want people to continue learning and

00:16:02.812 --> 00:16:05.672
continue working out or
monitoring what they eat.

00:16:05.672 --> 00:16:08.112
Those good behaviors should continue.

00:16:08.112 --> 00:16:12.266
So what we found in this research is
that it is important to think about goal

00:16:12.266 --> 00:16:16.290
success, not just as a destination,
but also as a journey.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:20.191
It doesn't actually have to be a journey
forward, thinking about the next goal.

00:16:20.191 --> 00:16:25.090
But it's actually more important to use it
as a journey to look backwards, to look at

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:29.644
what you have done right during this
process that helped you achieve the goal.

00:16:29.644 --> 00:16:33.040
Who were instrumental for
you during this journey?

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:34.650
&gt;&gt; Well, I've enjoyed this very much.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:35.469
Before we end,

00:16:35.469 --> 00:16:39.320
I'd like to ask you the same three
questions I ask everyone who joins me.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:40.128
Is that okay with you?

00:16:40.128 --> 00:16:40.885
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:16:40.885 --> 00:16:43.170
&gt;&gt; Great, then let's get started.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:47.483
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you've ever received

00:16:47.483 --> 00:16:52.220
as a five to seven-word presentation
slide title, what would it be?

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:56.000
&gt;&gt; It actually already came up earlier
in our talk and I just need three words,

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:57.860
know your audience.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.994
&gt;&gt; We have heard that
a lot on this podcast,

00:17:00.994 --> 00:17:05.890
and what you bring to that is
really a lot of depth and richness.

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:08.040
So it's about emotionality.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:11.135
It's about understanding that
audiences aren't homogenous,

00:17:11.135 --> 00:17:13.958
that there's differentiations and
nuance in there and I-

00:17:13.958 --> 00:17:15.322
&gt;&gt; And to know that they evolve,

00:17:15.322 --> 00:17:16.580
they will change.

00:17:16.580 --> 00:17:20.570
&gt;&gt; Right, over time, that journey
that's so important, it's not static.

00:17:20.570 --> 00:17:22.480
Excellent, thank you.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:25.850
Who's the communicator that you admire and
why?

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:27.486
&gt;&gt; I have to say Michelle Obama.

00:17:27.486 --> 00:17:32.654
And I think every time I hear her speak,
there are four things that comes to mind,

00:17:32.654 --> 00:17:36.510
clarity, confidence,
elegance, and empathy.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:40.560
And I think a speech that has all
four elements are very powerful.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.480
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and
you role model some of that yourself.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:45.668
I mean, the clarity of the information,

00:17:45.668 --> 00:17:49.620
the elegance of the research that
you've done there, excellent choice.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:51.891
Number three,

00:17:51.891 --> 00:17:58.320
what are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:01.875
&gt;&gt; So the first one, I have to come back
to the same point, know your audience.

00:18:01.875 --> 00:18:05.670
Specifically, know why they
are there to listen to the message.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:08.302
And the second one will be
to tailor your content,

00:18:08.302 --> 00:18:11.430
connecting your content to that audience.

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:13.700
And the third one, make it personal.

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:15.980
You also have to connect
the content to you.

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:19.034
So you're not just lecturing something
that they can hear from anybody else,

00:18:19.034 --> 00:18:22.080
they're actually learning
specifically from you.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:23.530
&gt;&gt; All three of those are critical.

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:26.904
I want to put an exclamation point after
that personal part because I think that's

00:18:26.904 --> 00:18:28.690
something that we don't think about, and

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:31.140
sometimes people don't
feel comfortable doing.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:34.488
But it really does make a difference if
you feel that the person has a direct

00:18:34.488 --> 00:18:37.310
connection to that content,
it's meaningful to them.

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:38.580
Thank you for reminding us of that.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:40.240
That's really, really important.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:43.105
Well, thank you, Szu-chi.

00:18:43.105 --> 00:18:45.090
This has been wonderful.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:49.766
You have certainly motivated me to
be more interested in motivation.

00:18:49.766 --> 00:18:53.632
I love your research and how it covers so
many different areas and

00:18:53.632 --> 00:18:58.500
really makes us think about who we're
talking to, when we're talking to them,

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:00.808
and how we can motivate our audience.

00:19:00.808 --> 00:19:01.624
Thank you so much.

00:19:01.624 --> 00:19:03.488
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for having me, Matt.

